1: #!/bin/bash
2: # GATE 13 -- PowerPC single-precision conversion fidelity, on real guest instructions.
3: #
4: # What this protects
5: # -----------------
6: # #304 gave `frsp` the rounding mode it never read (the narrowing was a host C cast,
7: # nearest-always, so the three directed modes were unreachable) and taught `stfs` the
8: # ISA's denormalization band, which it had been flushing to signed zero. #305 stopped
9: # `lfs`/`stfs` from destroying a NaN's sign and payload: the value-domain transport
10: # (interpret -> host double -> store) structurally cannot carry them, so the NaN classes
11: # are handled by bit surgery on the pattern instead.
12: #
13: # Why this gate had to exist BEFORE the fixes
14: # ------------------------------------------
15: # There is no PowerPC OS rig -- no guest boots on this path -- so the defects were found
16: # by a cold-debugger spike and every one of them could only be believed once an
17: # instrument measured it. The rows below were run against the COMMITTED build first and
18: # their committed bytes recorded; the fix then had to flip exactly the DISC rows and
19: # leave every PIN untouched. That baseline sweep is what makes "the fix worked" a
20: # measurement rather than a claim, and it caught three of the author's own wrong
21: # predictions before any code changed (frsp's RN overflow to Infinity is ISA-correct,
22: # and the negative band row keeps its sign through the flush).
23: #
24: # Three row classes, and why PINs are not decoration
25: # -------------------------------------------------
26: #   PIN  -- correct today, must stay. Includes two POLICY pins: for finite values at
27: #           and above ~2^129 the letter of Book I would splice-WRAP the exponent, and
28: #           this fork deliberately keeps #287's +/-Inf instead (the letter turns a
29: #           finite overflow into a NaN pattern: 1.5*2^128 -> 0x7FC00000). A policy pin
30: #           failing means someone changed a known divergence without updating the
31: #           record -- worth knowing either way.
32: #   DISC -- discriminator: committed byte is the defect, want flips with the fix.
33: #   DIV  -- known divergence left to a later round, pinned so it cannot drift.
34: #   [#556, 2026-10-02 -- a records correction; the text above is kept as it was written] The DISC classification
35: #   includes 7 negative controls in CAUSE_ROWS that verify non-raising of exception flags (yielding identical values
36: #   before and after #330), contrary to :32 defining DISC as values that flip with the fix.  (hbundle_regress27:F11:
37: #   regress/artifacts/astraretro_harness_20260926/HARNESS_DISPOSITIONS.md)
38: #
39: # #310's eighteen rows cover the eight float UPDATE forms, every one of which
40: # stopped the emulator before this round -- neither the primary opcodes
41: # 0x31/0x33/0x35/0x37 nor the indexed 567/631/695/759 were defined or decoded.
42: # Each form gets BOTH halves of its contract: a value row and a base-register
43: # row. A diff-review seat caught the first version giving five forms a base row
44: # only, which would have passed an implementation with the wrong transfer size
45: # that still advanced the base.
46: #
160: fi
161: 
162: # ---- ppcrldiclzero (#522) + #565: X(rldicl) wrote ZERO on every execution -- its 24-row detector --------
163: # Shipped EXEMPT with e777ff06 (2026-09-10) and re-dated twice by owner ruling (2026-09-24 with the
164: # other nine, then 04f3e7c0 on 2026-10-09) because no block here scored it; #565 discharges that
165: # exemption.  BEFORE the NetBSD prerequisite for the reason the rlwimi block gives: `-E bareppc -M 64`
166: # needs no image, so a red row is recorded first.  A stalled guest cannot hang the gate: the probe's
167: # own subprocess backstops (90 s; 60 s for the bogus-machine and 32-bit sessions) turn it into
168: # failing rows.  The probe prints no RESULT/CONTROL protocol, so it is scored as gate_arm.sh scores
169: # mk48's: its completion sentinel, then EVERY row by name, then the run/green/red counts and its own
170: # SCORE line, each pinned to a literal so a shrunk or renamed table cannot pass -- 30 checks when
171: # green.  Names sit in a %-18s column and none is longer than 15, so `^name  ' cannot match a
172: # neighbour (the padded-column trap).  The rows-run line is in the form check_score_claims.py pins.
173: # CONTRACT: the block TRANSMITS the probe's verdicts and re-derives none -- each row's ok/FAIL and its
174: # SCORE come from one comparison in the probe's row(), so an edit there (Grok 4.7's `ok = True', GPT-6
175: # Astra's `>=', #565 pass 2) lets a mutant through, as it would through any block that scores only a
176: # probe's ok/FAIL tokens (some gate_arm.sh blocks also pin a got= value; this one, like mk48's, does not).
177: # The exit status is ignored on purpose: this probe exits 0 with failed rows (probeexitzero).
178: # Floor (c), measured (regress/artifacts/ppcrldiclzero/gatewire_20261018/nullmedium/): an IDENTITY
179: # BODY for X(rldicl) -- decode and retire, write nothing -- reds 11 of the 30 (identity/), and
180: # /bin/true 22 of the 30.  An absent binary reds the sentinel check in this block's scoring text, but
181: # in gate 13 need_exec turns it into a SKIP before any check.
182: RLCLOG=$LOGDIR/ppc_rldicl.log
183: python3 -u ppc_rldicl_probe.py "$PMAX" gate > "$RLCLOG" 2>&1 || true
184: if [ "$(grep -cx 'PPC_RLDICL_PROBE_DONE' "$RLCLOG")" != 1 ]; then
185:     note "ppc_rldicl: completion sentinel missing or duplicated; last lines follow"
186:     tail -5 "$RLCLOG" | sed 's/^/       /'
187:     check "ppc_rldicl probe completed" "no" "yes"
188: else
189:     check "ppc_rldicl probe completed" "yes" "yes"
190:     for v in "session" "live" "seed holds" "src intact" "disasm xcheck" "zx32 rldicl" "zx32 rldicr" "rot8 rldicl" "rot8 rldicr" "naive fix wrong" "neg apparatus" "bits32 live" "bits32 rld bad" "vacuous cell" "vac neighbour" "census run" "census wrong" "census zero" "disasm total" "cand session" "cr parsed" "sh63 rldicl" "sh32 rldicl" "rc cr0"; do
191:         check "ppc_rldicl row: $v" "$(count "$RLCLOG" "^$v  .*ok$")" "1"
192:     done
193:     rlcok=$(count "$RLCLOG" " ok$"); rlcbad=$(count "$RLCLOG" " FAIL$"); rlcrun=$((rlcok + rlcbad))
194:     check "ppc_rldicl rows run"   "$rlcrun" 24
195:     check "ppc_rldicl rows green" "$rlcok" 24
196:     check "ppc_rldicl rows red"   "$rlcbad" 0
197:     #  EVERY SCORE line is counted, then the one is read: counting only `SCORE 24/24' lines let an
198:     #  appended contradictory `SCORE 0/24' pass (GPT-6 Astra, pass 1).
199:     check "ppc_rldicl score lines"  "$(grep -c '^SCORE ' "$RLCLOG")" "1"
200:     check "ppc_rldicl score 24/24"  "$(grep -c '^SCORE 24/24 ' "$RLCLOG")" "1"
201: fi
202: 
203: need_file "$IMAGES/netbsd401-macppc-GENERIC"
204: 
50:     case "${1-}:${2-}" in
51:     landisk:0)   echo absent ;;
52:     luna88k:0)   echo zeroed ;;
53:     *:)          echo unreported ;;
54:     luna88k:*)   if [ "$2" -ge 8174849291 ] 2>/dev/null && [ "$2" -le 15571141508 ]
55:                  then echo calibrated; else echo out-of-range; fi ;;
56:     *:0)         echo absent ;;
57:     *)           echo uncalibrated-rig ;;
58:     esac
59: }
60: 
61: C_OK=$'\033[32m'; C_BAD=$'\033[31m'; C_SKIP=$'\033[33m'; C_OFF=$'\033[0m'
62: [ -t 1 ] || { C_OK=""; C_BAD=""; C_SKIP=""; C_OFF=""; }
63: 
64: # Exit codes. SKIP is 77, NOT 2, because bash exits 2 on a SYNTAX ERROR -- so with 2 as
65: # the skip code a typo in any gate was silently downgraded to "coverage gap" and run.sh
66: # still exited 0. Anything non-zero that is not 77 is a failure.
67: EXIT_SKIP=77
68: 
69: GATE_NAME=""
70: _fails=0
71: _checks=0
72: 
73: gate_begin() { GATE_NAME=$1; _fails=0; _checks=0
74:     echo "############################################################"
75:     echo "## GATE: $GATE_NAME"
76:     echo "############################################################"
77: }
78: 
79: # check <description> <actual> <expected>
80: check() {
81:     _checks=$((_checks+1))
82:     if [ "$2" = "$3" ]; then
83:         printf "  ${C_OK}ok${C_OFF}   %-52s %s\n" "$1" "$2"
84:     else
85:         printf "  ${C_BAD}FAIL${C_OFF} %-52s got=%s want=%s\n" "$1" "$2" "$3"
86:         _fails=$((_fails+1))
87:     fi
88: }
89: 
90: # check_min <description> <actual> <minimum>
91: check_min() {
92:     _checks=$((_checks+1))
93:     if [ "$2" -ge "$3" ] 2>/dev/null; then
94:         printf "  ${C_OK}ok${C_OFF}   %-52s %s (>= %s)\n" "$1" "$2" "$3"
95:     else
96:         printf "  ${C_BAD}FAIL${C_OFF} %-52s got=%s want>=%s\n" "$1" "$2" "$3"
97:         _fails=$((_fails+1))
98:     fi
99: }
100: 
101: note() { printf "  --   %s\n" "$*"; }
102: 
103: # A gate may declare that part of its coverage did not run (a rig whose image is absent,
104: # say). That must not be absorbed into a PASS: it downgrades the whole gate to SKIP, so a
105: # missing input can never be reported as evidence of correctness.
106: _degraded=0
107: degrade() { _degraded=1; printf "  ${C_SKIP}skip${C_OFF} %s\n" "$*"; }
108: 
109: gate_end() {
110:     echo
111:     if [ "$_checks" = 0 ]; then
112:         echo "${C_SKIP}$GATE_NAME: SKIP${C_OFF} (no checks ran)"; return $EXIT_SKIP
113:     elif [ "$_fails" != 0 ]; then
114:         echo "${C_BAD}$GATE_NAME: FAIL${C_OFF} ($_fails of $_checks checks)"; return 1
115:     elif [ "$_degraded" != 0 ]; then
116:         echo "${C_SKIP}$GATE_NAME: SKIP${C_OFF} ($_checks checks passed, but part of the gate could not run)"
117:         return $EXIT_SKIP
118:     else
119:         echo "${C_OK}$GATE_NAME: PASS${C_OFF} ($_checks checks)"; return 0
120:     fi
121: }
122: 
123: #  #371: gate_skip() exits with the SKIP code, which run.sh maps to
124: #  REGRESS_PASS_WITH_GAPS -- a green-ish verdict. It never consulted the running
125: #  failure counter, so a gate that had ALREADY recorded red checks and then hit a
126: #  gate_skip (a probe that produced no result line, a missing rig image) reported
127: #  those reds as a skip and they vanished from the battery. Measured on a
128: #  synthetic gate: one FAIL then gate_skip exited 77, PASS_WITH_GAPS. This is the
129: #  same "a green row means nothing" class the gate rows themselves guard against,
130: #  in the harness's own control flow.
131: #
132: #  So a gate_skip AFTER failures is a FAIL, not a skip: the skip cannot un-record
133: #  what already failed. A gate_skip with a clean slate stays a genuine skip (a
134: #  preflight that could not run at all -- need_file/need_exec). degrade() was
135: #  already correct: gate_end() tests _fails before _degraded, so a degraded gate
136: #  with recorded failures already reports FAIL. Nothing there to change.
137: gate_skip() {
138:     echo
139:     if [ "${_fails:-0}" != 0 ]; then
140:         echo "${C_BAD}$GATE_NAME: FAIL${C_OFF} ($_fails of $_checks checks failed" \
141:              "before this section could not run: $*)"
142:         exit 1
143:     fi
144:     echo "${C_SKIP}$GATE_NAME: SKIP${C_OFF} -- $*"; exit $EXIT_SKIP
145: }
146: 
147: need_file() {
148:     for f in "$@"; do
149:         [ -e "$f" ] || gate_skip "missing required input: $f"
150:     done
151: }
152: 
153: need_exec() {
154:     for f in "$@"; do
155:         [ -x "$f" ] || gate_skip "missing or non-executable binary: $f"
156:     done
157: }
158: 
159: # run_emu <seconds> <logfile> <binary> [args...]
160: # Always unbuffered, always with stdin closed so a guest prompt cannot wedge the run.
161: run_emu() {
162:     local secs=$1 log=$2; shift 2
163:     ( cd "$IMAGES" 2>/dev/null || cd /; timeout "$secs" stdbuf -o0 -e0 "$@" </dev/null ) \
164:         > "$log" 2>&1
165:     return 0
166: }
167: 
168: # run_emu_progress <backstop_s> <budget_instrs> <stall_s> <marker_re> <logfile> <binary> [args...]
169: #
170: # #419: run_emu()'s wall-clock budget is a LOAD-SENSITIVE ORACLE. Under host load the guest
171: # makes less progress in the same seconds, the marker never appears, and the row FAILs
172: # INDISTINGUISHABLY FROM A REAL CAPABILITY REGRESSION -- measured twice, from panel seats and
173: # from interactive greps. This runs the emulator against a budget of GUEST WORK instead, and
174: # -- the part that actually fixes the defect -- it REPORTS WHY IT STOPPED.
175: #
320: # count <logfile> <pattern>   -- number of matching lines, 0 if the file is absent.
321: #
322: # NOT `[ -f x ] && grep -ac ... || echo 0`. On zero matches grep prints "0" AND exits 1,
323: # so the || arm fires too and the function emits TWO lines ("0\n0"). Measured: 3 bytes,
324: # 2 lines. That silently corrupted every marker string built from it, and made gate 6's
325: # one deliberate expected-negative assertion impossible to satisfy.
326: count() {
327:     [ -f "$1" ] || { echo 0; return; }
328:     local c
329:     c=$(grep -ac -- "$2" "$1" 2>/dev/null)
330:     echo "${c:-0}"
331: }

