    records: the biggest pile of unowned host kills is triaged, and the answer that matters is a ZERO and a "nothing drives this"
    
    The SCSI controller holds the largest single concentration in this project's unowned
    error-exit census.  A triage answered the two questions I most wanted answered, and both
    answers push AGAINST doing the work soon rather than for it.
    
    *** THE FILE HAS SIXTEEN EXITS, NOT THE FOURTEEN THE CENSUS LISTED -- AND THE TWO EXTRA
    ARE PRECISELY THE TWO THAT TURN OUT TO BE DEAD. ***  So the census enumerated exactly the
    live set without knowing why it had.  Fourteen are reachable from a guest register access
    or guest-started sequencer program; two are not.
    
    *** POPULATION THREE IS EMPTY, AND THAT ZERO IS WHAT LICENSES THE WHOLE BODY OF WORK. ***
    NONE of the sixteen checks establishes corrupted host ownership or any other condition
    that requires terminating.  The seat says plainly that the alarming diagnostic wording --
    "cannot be combined" -- does not turn these into internal assertions.  That is an argument
    a round would otherwise have had to construct sixteen separate times.
    
    THE TWO DEAD PATHS ARE RECORDED WITH THEIR WARRANTS, one of them mathematical: the outer
    instruction-class default masks with a two-bit value and explicitly handles all four
    results, so it cannot be reached at all.  The other is dead by a chain of phase reasoning
    -- AND ONLY WHILE THE REGISTER DEFAULT IS STILL FATAL.  A direct write to the phase
    register terminates at that default before another sequencer program can run.  **SO FIXING
    THE REGISTER DEFAULT COULD REVIVE THE DEAD PATH**, and a round taking the sites in the
    obvious priority order would not see that coming.
    
    A SECOND TRAP ON THE SAME FIRST ROW: the byte path stores BEFORE dispatch and the word path
    also stores to two more registers before reaching the unhandled-register default, so
    restoring one saved value would not undo the word stores.  A one-line repair here is wrong
    in two different ways.
    
    *** AND THE SEVERITY CEILING, ASKED FOR AND ANSWERED WITHOUT HEDGING: "no in-tree runnable
    workload drives this controller's register/sequencer interface." ***  All fourteen are
    reachable in principle and NOTHING IN THIS TREE EXERCISES THEM.  That is worth more than a
    ranking: these rounds cannot reach a boot witness, and a witness would have to plant
    sequencer programs by hand.
    
    The triage also corrected the census's own effort ranking -- two of the nine prescribed
    rows are cheaper than the blanket "preserve command termination, status and interrupt
    behaviour" implied, because the real status sequence lives elsewhere and the backend
    failure branches were already de-fatalised by earlier rounds.
    
    I have NOT filed the nine rows.  Filing nine for a controller nothing drives would crowd a
    queue that already holds live guest-reachable kills on machines this project does boot.
    The triage is recorded against the tracking row so the next session inherits the analysis
    rather than re-deriving it.
    
    *** AND THE SECOND TRIAGE REFUTED MY WORKING HYPOTHESIS, WHICH IS THE USEFUL OUTCOME. ***
    I asked which of the seven remaining coprocessor exits share the shape of the one already
    filed -- the value computed and then thrown away -- on the theory that several would
    collapse into one cheap round.  NONE OF THEM DOES.  That stops a round copying a
    disposition across a file on the strength of one precedent, which is exactly what the
    option-set rule warns about when a warrant is transferred without checking each site.
    
    It also found the census undercounted that file: eight ACTIVE exits plus SIX COMMENTED-OUT
    ones.  The dead six are not defects, but a round de-fatalising a live site sits beside six
    places where somebody already made that call and left the remains in.
    
    The structure decides the split.  Two of the seven are SHARED ENDPOINTS fed from three and
    four separate defaults, so one repair at each covers many decode paths at once -- those
    rank first.  One is neither a case nor a default but an ENTRY GUARD firing before any
    dispatch, which no other member of this class has been, and it ranks last for that reason
    rather than for unimportance.
    
    BOTH OF TODAY'S LARGE TRIAGES CAME BACK ARGUING AGAINST DOING THE WORK SOON.  One found
    nothing in-tree drives the device at all; the other found the cheap-repair theory does not
    hold.  Neither is the answer I wanted and both save a round from starting on a false
    premise.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
